# Citation metadata, CFF 1.2.0 (https://citation-file-format.github.io).
# GitHub reads this file directly and renders a "Cite this repository" button.
#
# The author list is kept identical to `[project].authors` in
# pyproject.toml, which is the ratified list. Edit both together, in the same
# order, or the package metadata and the citation disagree about who wrote this.
#
# Deliberately ABSENT, because inventing any of these would be worse than
# omitting them:
#   * `doi:` -- no DOI has been minted. Add one (Zenodo archives a GitHub
#     release automatically) when v0.1.0 is tagged, and add a matching
#     `identifiers:` entry here.
#   * `preferred-citation:` -- there is no paper or preprint yet. When one
#     exists, add it and citation tools will prefer it over this software
#     entry.
#   * `affiliation:` on each author -- the repository states no name-to-
#     institution mapping. Only a collective sentence exists (CONTRIBUTING.md:
#     "researchers at Argonne National Laboratory and collaborating
#     institutions"). Deriving affiliations from email domains would be a
#     guess published under the authors' names, and two of the domains cannot
#     be expanded from anything in the repo at all. Authors should add their
#     own, along with `orcid:`, which is likewise absent everywhere.
#   * `date-released:` -- nothing has been tagged. CHANGELOG.md has only an
#     `[Unreleased]` section, and no semantic-version tag exists. Add it with
#     the v0.1.0 tag.
#
# The repository carries two expansions of the acronym: this one, used by
# README.md, docs/index.md and NOTICE, and "Foundational Multimodal Training
# Framework", used by pyproject's one-line description and the CLI help. The
# citation follows NOTICE, since that is the file the copyright line lives in.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use PRISM in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "PRISM: Poly-Reasoning Integrated Scientific Multimodal Modeling Framework"
abstract: >-
  PRISM is a framework for training and evaluating multimodal transformers over
  scientific data. A pretrained language backbone is extended with per-modality
  encoders and trainable projectors, so that text, images, time series, DNA
  sequences, graphs, tables, and geometry can be represented in one token
  stream. It targets DOE leadership-class systems, with Intel Max Series GPUs
  on Aurora as the most exercised system to date. Support for further systems is
  expected.
type: software
version: 0.1.0
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/AI-ModCon/BaseMM_PRISM"
url: "https://github.com/AI-ModCon/BaseMM_PRISM"
keywords:
  - multimodal
  - machine learning
  - scientific computing
  - transformers
  - high-performance computing
